g and leave pre-tax retirement at $0. The standard deduction leaves $43,900 of taxable income. Federal income tax comes to $5,020, while Social Security takes $3,720 and Medicare takes $870. Together, those payroll taxes are $4,590. Subtract federal and payroll tax from gross pay to get $50,390 for the year. Across 26 biweekly checks, that is $1,938.08 per check, or 84.0% of gross pay. State tax, credits and other withholding could change what appears on a pay stub. Share Interactive — edit any field How Take-Home Pay Is Calculated The paycheck calculator estimates annual take-home pay, then divides it by the number of checks in your pay schedule. Annual take-home pay equals gross pay minus pre-tax retirement contributions, federal income tax, Social Security and Medicare. For the $60,000 single-filer example, the result is $50,390 a year and $1,938.08 across 26 biweekly checks. You can enter gross pay by year or by paycheck. The schedule choices are weekly, every two weeks, twice a month and monthly, with 52, 26, 24 and 12 checks respectively. If you need to convert a pay figure before entering it, use the salary calculator . Federal income tax starts with taxable income: gross pay minus your annual pre-tax retirement contribution and the standard deduction, with a floor of zero. The calculator applies the Internal Revenue Service (IRS) 2026 brackets band by band to that amount. For a single filer earning $60,000, taxable income is $43,900. The first $12,400 is taxed at 10%, producing $1,240. The remaining $31,500 is taxed at 12%, producing $3,780. Those bands add up to $5,020 in federal income tax. Being in a higher bracket does not apply that bracket's rate to every dollar of taxable income. Social Security is 6.2% of gross wages up to the $184,500 wage base. Medicare is 1.45% of all gross wages, and employers withhold an additional 0.9% on wages above $200,000 in a calendar year. Neither tax shrinks when you make a pre-tax retirement contribution. For example, Social Security on a $300,000 salary stops at $11,439, while Medicare comes to $5,250. On $250,000, Medicare is $4,075, which includes $450 in Additional Medicare withholding. The calculator shows Social Security and Medicare separately. A pre-tax retirement contribution lowers the income used to calculate federal income tax, but it does not lower the gross wages used for Social Security or Medicare. On a $100,000 single-filer salary, a $10,000 contribution reduces federal income tax from $13,170 to $10,970. Social Security stays at $6,200, and Medicare stays at $1,450. Annual take-home pay is $71,380 with the contribution, compared with $79,180 without it. The contribution lowers take-home pay by $7,800 because federal income tax falls by $2,200. Enter the contribution as an annual amount, even if you enter gross pay per paycheck. 2026 Federal Figures Used The paycheck calculator uses the following 2026 standard deductions, income tax brackets and payroll tax figures. Item 2026 figure Source Standard deduction, single $16,100 IRS 2026 adjustments Standard deduction, married filing jointly $32,200 IRS 2026 adjustments Standard deduction, head of household $24,150 IRS 2026 adjustments Income tax rates 10%, 12%, 22%, 24%, 32%, 35%, 37% IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-32 Single, top of the 10% and 12% brackets $12,400 and $50,400 of taxable income IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-32 Social Security tax 6.2% up to $184,500 of wages IRS Topic 751 Medicare tax 1.45% of all wages IRS Topic 751 Additional Medicare withholding 0.9% of wages above $200,000 IRS Topic 751 For more detail on where taxable income falls, use the federal tax bracket calculator . The figures above were checked on the linked IRS pages on 2026-11-15. Take-Home Pay at Different Salaries In our internal modeling, we ran 2026 federal brackets and payroll tax across salaries from $40,000 to $150,000 to estimate take-home pay. The table assumes a single filer taking the standard deduction, with no state tax, retirement contribution