itional Medicare withholding above $200,000 for every filing status. Final Additional Medicare liability uses different thresholds, including $250,000 for married filing jointly, as explained in IRS Topic 560 . A married couple's final liability can therefore differ from the Additional Medicare amount withheld. This paycheck calculator is not suited to self-employed or 1099 income, multiple jobs or itemized deductions. For self-employed or 1099 income, use the self-employment tax calculator . For multiple jobs or itemized deductions, use the IRS Tax Withholding Estimator . State tax, credits or extra W-4 withholding would also change the answer, so treat the result as an estimate rather than personalized withholding advice. Enter your gross pay and filing status in the paycheck calculator above, then compare its result with your pay stub. Frequently asked questions How much of my paycheck will I keep after taxes? At $60,000 a year, a single filer keeps an estimated $50,390 before state tax. That is $1,938.08 per biweekly check, or 84.0% of gross pay. The estimate subtracts federal income tax, Social Security and Medicare. Enter your own pay in the calculator above because filing status and pre-tax retirement contributions can change the result. Is a paycheck calculator accurate? It is an estimate of annual federal tax liability and take-home pay under the listed assumptions. It uses the standard deduction and does not calculate an employer's exact withholding under IRS Publication 15-T. State tax, credits and health premiums are also left out. Compare the per-check result with a pay stub if you need to understand a difference. How do I calculate my paycheck manually? Start with annual gross pay and subtract pre-tax retirement contributions and the standard deduction to find taxable income. Apply the federal brackets to taxable income band by band. Then subtract federal income tax, Social Security, Medicare and the retirement contribution from gross pay. Divide annual take-home pay by the number of checks in your chosen pay schedule. Does this paycheck calculator include state tax? No. The paycheck calculator does not subtract state or local income tax. Its take-home figure is therefore before those taxes. If state or local tax appears on your pay stub, that is one reason the actual net check can be lower than this estimate. Why is my real paycheck different from this estimate? Your employer's withholding can differ from this annual-liability estimate. The calculator also leaves out state tax, credits, health premiums and extra W-4 withholding. It does not model tips or overtime deductions. Compare the deduction rows with your pay stub to identify which amounts the estimate leaves out. How does a 401(k) contribution change my take-home pay? A pre-tax retirement contribution reduces the income used for federal income tax, while Social Security and Medicare still use gross wages. In the $100,000 single-filer case, a $10,000 contribution lowers federal income tax by $2,200. Take-home pay falls by $7,800 rather than the full contribution amount. Enter your annual contribution to see the change in the calculator. Why does Social Security tax stop on high salaries? Social Security tax applies only up to the 2026 wage base of $184,500. The employee rate is 6.2%, so the modeled Social Security tax on a $300,000 salary is $11,439. Medicare still applies to gross wages above the Social Security wage base. The calculator shows both taxes separately in its results. Does a paycheck calculator include Medicare tax? Yes. This paycheck calculator subtracts Medicare tax at 1.45% of gross wages. It also models an extra 0.9% withheld on wages above $200,000. Final Additional Medicare liability can use a different threshold for married filing jointly, so that withholding estimate may differ from the final tax. Sources We prioritize primary sources for rules, formulas, rates, limits, and definitions. See our calculator methodology and editorial pol